    "question": {
      ":question/id": "00b74cef-26cc-5aef-9aad-a5d9ffe4332a",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nX+Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Knowing that the matrix representation $T=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{11} & {t}_{12} \\\\ {t}_{21} & {t}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$ of a linear transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=XZ+YZ,\n$$\n\nfind the values ${t}_{ij}.$\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n${t}_{11}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-1}}                \n\n\n\n\n\n$\\,{t}_{12}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-2}}                \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n${t}_{21}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-3}}                \n\n\n\n\n\n$\\,{t}_{22}=$\n\n\n\n\n\n{{field-4}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nXZ+YZ=(X+Y)Z.\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =XZ+YZ \\\\  & =(X+Y)Z \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
